        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofdepue.gov/history",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before becoming DePue, the village was called Trenton and grew around a 1835 warehouse that turned the Illinois River into a steamboat hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/depue_illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A 1905 zinc smelter sparked an industrial boom, later hosting sulfuric acid, fertilizer, and metal processing until 1989.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/depue_illinois",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lake DePue, a 531‑acre oxbow lake formed from a former Illinois River meander, borders the village to the west.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/depue_illinois",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every year, the American Power Boat Association hosts a weekend of boat races on Lake DePue that pulls crowds from across the country.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
